RISE ASCII SCENE 7
#############################################################################################################
#                                      RISE Ascii Scene File                                                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   File:           weathered_reliquary.RISEscene                                                          #
#   Description:    "The Weathered Reliquary" -- the canonical showcase for the geometry-derived shading   #
#                   signals (curv/curvR, occlusion(r), thickness(r)) shipped in                             #
#                   docs/GEOMETRY_SHADING_SIGNALS_DESIGN.md.  A dusk-lit museum tableau on one carved       #
#                   stone plinth, path traced (signals are fully correct only under PT -- see the           #
#                   descriptor text on expression_painter/scalar_painter for the BDPT/VCM/MLT residual).    #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   Five things to look at:                                                                 #
#                     1. THE BRONZE GUARDIAN (skeleton_geometry, an SDF): curv drives bright polished-edge  #
#                        wear on convex ridges and, on the concave side, a deep green-brown patina that     #
#                        occlusion(r) deepens specifically in the enclosed folds (armpits, groin, the horn  #
#                        roots) rather than on shallow, open concavities.  Base colour, metallic and        #
#                        roughness are all driven by ONE shared scalar field -- "wear follows the form",    #
#                        not a world-axis proxy.  curv is EXACT here (a true differential quantity on an    #
#                        implicit surface).                                                                 #
#                     2. THE CARVED STONE PLINTH (sdf_geometry, box + roundbox CSG subtraction): soot and   #
#                        dust pool in the recessed panels and grooves via occlusion + concave curv; the     #
#                        swept flat faces stay clean.  This is the CSG-subtraction cavity path.             #
#                     3. THE ALABASTER VOTIVE SHELL (sdf_geometry, sphere-minus-offset-sphere): a genuinely #
#                        varying wall -- thin at the rim, thick at the base -- lit from within by a small   #
#                        warm point.  1-thickness(r) drives BOTH the transmittance and the reflectance of a #
#                        translucent_material, so the thin rim glows warm and the thick base stays opaque   #
#                        alabaster.  This is the thickness showcase and the scene's emotional centerpiece.  #
#                     4. THREE SCALES OF THE SAME GUARDIAN (standard_object at scale 1.0 / 0.5 / 0.3,       #
#                        sharing one geometry and one material): the wear pattern sits in the SAME places   #
#                        on the shape at every scale, because curv's scaleHint folds in the object's own    #
#                        world scale (docs/GEOMETRY_SHADING_SIGNALS_DESIGN.md section 5.2) and              #
#                        occlusion()/thickness() are already posed in the geometry's own object space with  #
#                        a dimensionless radius.  The wear field's fbm breakup is keyed on `Po` (OBJECT      #
#                        position), not `P` (world position), for the same reason -- a world-space noise    #
#                        field would NOT repeat identically across instances placed at different world      #
#                        positions even if curv itself is scale-correct.                                    #
#                     5. THE VOTIVE GOBLET (lathe_geometry -> a baked ITriangleMeshGeometryIndexed): edge    #
#                        wear via curv (faceted here -- only as good as the vertex normals, per the         #
#                        design doc's mesh caveat) plus crevice dirt via occlusion() at the stem/knop,      #
#                        exercising the MESH per-vertex bake path.  The occlusion() radius on this object   #
#                        is a bare numeric LITERAL (0.05), not a `param` reference -- a mesh answers from a #
#                        table baked at one fixed radius, and only a literal radius can be proven constant  #
#                        at parse time.  Watch RISE_Log.txt for the "MeshSignalBake" line the first time    #
#                        this scene renders; every later render (same radius) reads the cached table.       #
#                                                                                                           #
#                   Every art-directable number below is a `param` with min/max/step/label.                #
#                                                                                                           #
#   Author:         RISE contributors                                                                       #
#   Birthdate:      2026-08-30                                                                              #
#                                                                                                           #
#############################################################################################################

##################################################
# Renderer -- Path Tracing.  Signals (curv,
# occlusion, thickness) are fully correct only
# under PT; BDPT/VCM/MLT evaluate them as their
# neutral fallback in parts of their transport
# (design doc Phase-2 known-residual paragraph),
# so this showcase does NOT use BDPT/VCM.
##################################################

# Declared before the rasterizer (which references it as `radiance_map`) --
# a dim, cool gallery ambience so rays that escape the tableau don't fall
# into a black void or a blown-out white one.
uniformcolor_painter
{
	name		pnt_gallery_ambient
	color		0.045 0.052 0.068
}

standard_shader
{
	name		global
	shaderop	DefaultPathTracing
}

pathtracing_pel_rasterizer
{
	samples					128
	oidn_denoise			TRUE
	radiance_map			pnt_gallery_ambient
	radiance_background		TRUE
}

# HDR primary -- verbatim radiance, source of truth.
file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern				rendered/weathered_reliquary
	type				EXR
	color_space			Rec709RGB_Linear
	exr_compression		piz
}

# PNG display preview -- ACES rolloff for a normal display.
file_rasterizeroutput
{
	pattern				rendered/weathered_reliquary
	type				PNG
	bpp					8
	color_space			sRGB
	exposure			0.0
	display_transform	aces
}

##################################################
# Film + Camera
##################################################

film
{
	width		800
	height		600
}

pinhole_camera
{
	location	0.15 1.55 4.40
	lookat		0.05 0.80 -0.05
	up			0 1 0
	fov			44.0
}

##################################################
# 1. THE BRONZE GUARDIAN -- skeleton_geometry
#
# 18 joints: pelvis/chest/neck/head root chain, two
# horns, two arms (shoulder-elbow-hand), two legs
# (hip-knee-foot, thighs/shins flattened with
# aspect 0.6 per the skill's "flatten a thigh to
# ~0.6" guidance).  Feet sit at local y=0 by
# construction, so any standard_object at this
# geometry with position.y=0 stands flush on
# whatever surface is at that height, at ANY scale
# -- this is what makes the three-scale instancing
# below (section 4) trivial to place correctly.
##################################################

skeleton_geometry
{
	name		geo_guardian
	blend		0.40
	joint		pelvis      none        0.00  0.62  0.00   0.190
	joint		chest       pelvis      0.00  0.90  0.02   0.210
	joint		neck        chest       0.00  1.05  0.05   0.120
	joint		head        neck        0.00  1.18  0.09   0.170
	joint		horn_l      head       -0.09  1.34  0.04   0.045
	joint		horn_r      head        0.09  1.34  0.04   0.045
	joint		shoulder_l  chest      -0.23  0.86  0.00   0.100
	joint		elbow_l     shoulder_l -0.33  0.63  0.06   0.075
	joint		hand_l      elbow_l    -0.30  0.42  0.12   0.065
	joint		shoulder_r  chest       0.23  0.86  0.00   0.100
	joint		elbow_r     shoulder_r  0.33  0.63  0.06   0.075
	joint		hand_r      elbow_r     0.30  0.42  0.12   0.065
	joint		hip_l       pelvis     -0.13  0.55  0.00   0.115  0.6
	joint		knee_l      hip_l      -0.15  0.30  0.04   0.095  0.6
	joint		foot_l      knee_l     -0.15  0.00  0.14   0.085
	joint		hip_r       pelvis      0.13  0.55  0.00   0.115  0.6
	joint		knee_r      hip_r       0.15  0.30  0.04   0.095  0.6
	joint		foot_r      knee_r      0.15  0.00  0.14   0.085
}

# THE SHARED WEAR FIELD.  `curv` alone gives fine-scale, radius-free edge
# detection; adding a `fbm(Po*...)` breakup keeps the mask from reading as
# an obviously-procedural smooth AO ramp (the tell of a lazy wear pass, per
# skills/agent/materials-and-media-basics.md), and multiplying the crevice
# side by an occlusion-derived `cavity_boost` deepens patina specifically in
# folds that are genuinely ENCLOSED (armpits, the horn roots, where the
# guardian's own limbs shadow themselves) versus a shallow, open concavity
# that curv alone would treat identically.  Because `edge_wear` and
# `crevice` share the SAME `breakup` term, a flat patch with high-frequency
# breakup bumps both up together and cancels out in the final difference --
# breakup only matters where curv has already committed to a sign.
#
# NOTE: fbm is keyed on `Po` (OBJECT position), not `P` (world position).
# `curv`, `occlusion()` and `thickness()` are already instance-scale-correct
# (design doc section 5.2 / section 6.1), but a world-space noise field is
# NOT -- three placements of this same geometry at different world
# positions would each see a different slice of a `P`-keyed field even
# though the underlying shape is identical.  `Po` ties the breakup pattern
# to the OBJECT's own frame, so it repeats identically across the three
# scaled instances in section 4.
expression_painter
{
	name		pnt_idol_wear_axis
	# NOTE ON MAGNITUDE: curv is normalized by the hit geometry's WHOLE
	# bounding-box diagonal (design doc section 5.2), and this guardian's
	# individual limbs/joints have a local radius (0.045-0.21) much
	# smaller than its own ~1.75-unit bbox diagonal -- so curv reads
	# roughly bboxDiag/localRadius ~= 8-40 on this creature's own convex
	# masses, not O(1).  Gains below are correspondingly small (< 1, not
	# the 3-30 that would suit a creature whose limbs are a large
	# fraction of its own bbox); this is the exact "0.05-unit radius on a
	# large creature" hazard the design doc's units section names.
	param		edge_gain    0.05 min 0.02 max 2    step 0.01 label "Edge polish sensitivity"
	param		crevice_gain 0.08 min 0.02 max 2    step 0.01 label "Crevice patina sensitivity"
	param		breakup_freq 14.0 min 1    max 60   step 0.5  label "Patina breakup frequency"
	param		occl_gain    1.6  min 0    max 4    step 0.1  label "Fold occlusion boost"
	param		occl_radius  0.08 min 0.02 max 0.30 step 0.01 label "Occlusion sample radius (bbox fraction)"
	def			breakup      0.35 * fbm(Po * breakup_freq, 4, 0.5, 2.0)
	def			edge_wear    clamp(curv * edge_gain + breakup, 0, 1)
	def			crevice      clamp(-curv * crevice_gain + breakup, 0, 1)
	def			cavity_boost 1.0 + occl_gain * (1.0 - occlusion(occl_radius))
	def			patina       clamp(crevice * cavity_boost, 0, 1)
	expr		0.5 + 0.5 * clamp(edge_wear - patina, -1, 1)
}

# Three consumers of the ONE field above, via ramp_painter (a Colour-pipe
# painter, so it binds cleanly to pbr_metallic_roughness_material's
# metallic/roughness slots -- those slots resolve through the COLOUR
# painter manager only; a scalar_painter name there is silently NOT found
# and synthesizes a zero-valued painter instead of binding (see the
# resolveOrSynth note on pbr_metallic_roughness_material's `metallic` /
# `roughness` parameters in ChunkParserRegistry.cpp).  ramp_painter sidesteps
# that trap entirely.
ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_idol_basecolor
	input			pnt_idol_wear_axis
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.025 0.050 0.032
	stop			0.50  0.260 0.165 0.078
	stop			1.00  0.620 0.470 0.220
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_idol_metallic
	input			pnt_idol_wear_axis
	channel			R
	interpolation	linear
	stop			0.00  0.22 0.22 0.22
	stop			1.00  0.92 0.92 0.92
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_idol_roughness
	input			pnt_idol_wear_axis
	channel			R
	interpolation	linear
	stop			0.00  0.58 0.58 0.58
	stop			1.00  0.10 0.10 0.10
}

pbr_metallic_roughness_material
{
	name		mat_idol
	base_color	pnt_idol_basecolor
	metallic	pnt_idol_metallic
	roughness	pnt_idol_roughness
}

##################################################
# 2. THE CARVED STONE PLINTH -- sdf_geometry
#
# One box slab (half-extents 1.10 x 0.18 x 0.78,
# spanning local y in [0, 0.36] so its TOP sits at
# object-space y=0.36) with three roundbox recessed
# panels and two long roundbox grooves subtracted
# from the front face.  Small subtract `k` gives
# the recess edges a soft chisel fillet rather than
# a knife-sharp CSG seam.
##################################################

sdf_geometry
{
	name		geo_reliquary_base
	part		box      union     0      0.00 0.18 0.00    0 0 0   1 1 1   1.10 0.18 0.78    0
	part		roundbox subtract  0.02  -0.65 0.18 0.70    0 0 0   1 1 1   0.28 0.09 0.10    0.02
	part		roundbox subtract  0.02   0.00 0.18 0.70    0 0 0   1 1 1   0.28 0.09 0.10    0.02
	part		roundbox subtract  0.02   0.65 0.18 0.70    0 0 0   1 1 1   0.28 0.09 0.10    0.02
	part		roundbox subtract  0.01   0.00 0.32 0.70    0 0 0   1 1 1   1.05 0.018 0.10   0.01
	part		roundbox subtract  0.01   0.00 0.06 0.70    0 0 0   1 1 1   1.05 0.018 0.10   0.01
}

# Soot/dust field: concave curv (a fine seam) plus real occlusion (a wide,
# shallow recessed panel curv alone underrates) -- occlusion is the term
# that actually lights up the three recessed panels and two grooves; curv
# only catches their sharp inner corners.
expression_painter
{
	name		pnt_base_grime
	param		crevice_gain 6.0  min 1    max 20   step 0.5  label "Base crevice sensitivity"
	param		occl_gain    0.85 min 0    max 2    step 0.05 label "Base soot/occlusion boost"
	param		occl_radius  0.05 min 0.01 max 0.20 step 0.01 label "Base occlusion sample radius (bbox fraction)"
	def			crevice clamp(-curv * crevice_gain, 0, 1)
	def			cavity  clamp(1.0 - occlusion(occl_radius), 0, 1)
	expr		clamp(crevice * 0.45 + cavity * occl_gain, 0, 1)
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_base_color
	input			pnt_base_grime
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.19  0.185 0.17
	stop			1.00  0.022 0.020 0.019
}

scalar_painter
{
	name		sp_base_rough
	painter		pnt_base_grime
	channel		R
	scale		0.20
	bias		0.55
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name	pnt_base_spec
	color	0.04 0.04 0.04
}

ggx_material
{
	name			mat_reliquary_base
	rd				pnt_base_color
	rs				pnt_base_spec
	alphax			sp_base_rough
	alphay			sp_base_rough
	fresnel_mode	schlick_f0
}

##################################################
# 3. THE ALABASTER VOTIVE SHELL -- sdf_geometry
#
# Outer sphere (radius 0.50, centered at local
# origin) minus an inner sphere (radius 0.30,
# centered 0.15 units HIGHER) -- an off-centre
# CSG subtraction gives a continuously varying wall
# thickness: 0.05 at the top pole (thin rim), 0.35
# at the bottom pole (thick base), ~0.22 at the
# equator.  A small subtract k=0.015 softens the
# interior seam without perturbing the estimator
# enough to matter (design doc section 6.2's "minor,
# unlike the heightfield case" caveat).
##################################################

sdf_geometry
{
	name		geo_votive_shell
	part		sphere union    0      0.00 0.00 0.00   0 0 0   1 1 1   0.50 0 0   0
	part		sphere subtract 0.015  0.00 0.15 0.00   0 0 0   1 1 1   0.30 0 0   0
}

# thickness(radius) returns [0,1] with 1=thick; 1-thickness is the
# "thinness" mask a rim-glow wants.  `thick_radius` is a `param`, evaluated
# LIVE on this SDF (no mesh-bake literal restriction applies here).
expression_painter
{
	name		pnt_shell_thinness
	param		thick_radius 0.12 min 0.03 max 0.35 step 0.01 label "Thickness sample radius (bbox fraction)"
	expr		clamp(1.0 - thickness(thick_radius), 0, 1)
}

# Reflectance DROPS and transmittance RISES together toward the thin rim
# -- more of the incident light continues on through rather than
# bouncing back, which is what makes the rim look lit from within instead
# of merely "less white".  Per-channel sums stay comfortably under 1.0 at
# both ends (translucent_material's own auto-scale safety net would catch
# a violation regardless, but only for uniformcolor_painter inputs -- these
# are ramp_painters, so the by-hand check matters here).
ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_shell_ref
	input			pnt_shell_thinness
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.42  0.39  0.33
	stop			1.00  0.06  0.05  0.045
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_shell_tau
	input			pnt_shell_thinness
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.015 0.012 0.008
	stop			1.00  0.400 0.280 0.140
}

translucent_material
{
	name		mat_votive_shell
	ref			pnt_shell_ref
	tau			pnt_shell_tau
	ext			0.0
	N			24.0
	scattering	0.0
}

##################################################
# 5. THE VOTIVE GOBLET -- lathe_geometry
#
# A turned foot / slender stem / knop / bowl
# profile, bakes to an ITriangleMeshGeometryIndexed.
# This is the MESH per-vertex bake exercise: curv
# is faceted (only as good as the vertex normals)
# and occlusion()'s radius below is a bare LITERAL
# (0.05), not a param -- see the header comment for
# why a mesh cannot accept a computed radius.
##################################################

lathe_geometry
{
	name			geo_goblet
	axis			y
	smooth			TRUE
	profile_point	0.000 0.000
	profile_point	0.150 0.015
	profile_point	0.150 0.030
	profile_point	0.070 0.055
	profile_point	0.032 0.075
	profile_point	0.030 0.220
	profile_point	0.055 0.245
	profile_point	0.030 0.270
	profile_point	0.038 0.300
	profile_point	0.130 0.335
	profile_point	0.215 0.370
	profile_point	0.205 0.430
	profile_point	0.220 0.460
	profile_point	0.210 0.470
}

expression_painter
{
	name		pnt_goblet_wear_axis
	param		edge_gain 0.08 min 0.02 max 20  step 0.01 label "Goblet edge polish sensitivity"
	param		dirt_gain 1.2  min 0.2 max 10  step 0.1  label "Goblet crevice dirt sensitivity"
	def			edge_wear clamp(curv * edge_gain, 0, 1)
	# LITERAL radius (0.05), required: geo_goblet is a triangle mesh, and
	# occlusion()/thickness() on a mesh answer from a per-vertex field baked
	# LAZILY at one fixed radius proven constant at PARSE time.  A
	# `param`-derived radius cannot be proven constant and would silently
	# read the neutral fallback (occlusion == 1, i.e. no dirt at all) --
	# see docs/GEOMETRY_SHADING_SIGNALS_DESIGN.md section 7.1.
	def			crevice_dirt clamp((1.0 - occlusion(0.05)) * dirt_gain, 0, 1)
	expr		0.5 + 0.5 * clamp(edge_wear - crevice_dirt, -1, 1)
}

ramp_painter
{
	name			pnt_goblet_basecolor
	input			pnt_goblet_wear_axis
	channel			R
	interpolation	smooth
	stop			0.00  0.028 0.034 0.024
	stop			0.50  0.240 0.155 0.070
	stop			1.00  0.620 0.470 0.200
}

scalar_painter
{
	name		sp_goblet_rough
	painter		pnt_goblet_wear_axis
	channel		R
	scale		-0.45
	bias		0.55
}

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name	pnt_goblet_spec
	color	0.58 0.50 0.34
}

ggx_material
{
	name		mat_goblet
	rd			pnt_goblet_basecolor
	rs			pnt_goblet_spec
	alphax		sp_goblet_rough
	alphay		sp_goblet_rough
	ior			1.18
	extinction	2.8
}

##################################################
# Floor
##################################################

uniformcolor_painter
{
	name	pnt_floor
	color	0.045 0.050 0.062
}

lambertian_material
{
	name		mat_floor
	reflectance	pnt_floor
}

infiniteplane_geometry
{
	name	geo_floor
	xtile	2.0
	ytile	2.0
}

standard_object
{
	name		obj_floor
	geometry	geo_floor
	material	mat_floor
	position	0 0 0
	orientation	-90 0 0
}

##################################################
# Objects -- everything below rests on top of the
# plinth (world y=0.36) except the plinth itself
# (world y=0, standing on the floor) and the floor.
##################################################

standard_object
{
	name		obj_reliquary_base
	geometry	geo_reliquary_base
	material	mat_reliquary_base
	position	0 0 0
}

# 1. Hero guardian, scale 1.0.
standard_object
{
	name		obj_guardian_hero
	geometry	geo_guardian
	material	mat_idol
	position	0.00 0.36 -0.15
}

# 4. Two smaller instances of the SAME geometry + material, at scale 0.5
# and 0.3.  Feet stay at local y=0 regardless of scale (by construction --
# every joint chain in geo_guardian was authored with the feet at y=0), so
# position.y=0.36 (the plinth top) is correct for all three without any
# per-scale offset math.
standard_object
{
	name		obj_guardian_half
	geometry	geo_guardian
	material	mat_idol
	position	-0.75 0.36 0.42
	scale		0.5 0.5 0.5
}

standard_object
{
	name		obj_guardian_third
	geometry	geo_guardian
	material	mat_idol
	position	0.80 0.36 0.30
	scale		0.3 0.3 0.3
}

# 3. Votive shell.  Local bottom pole sits at y=-0.50, so position.y=0.86
# (plinth top 0.36 + 0.50) rests it flush on the plinth.
standard_object
{
	name		obj_votive_shell
	geometry	geo_votive_shell
	material	mat_votive_shell
	position	-0.55 0.86 -0.20
}

# 5. Votive goblet.  Local bottom pole is at y=0.000 (the lathe profile's
# first point), so position.y=0.36 rests its foot flush on the plinth.
standard_object
{
	name		obj_goblet
	geometry	geo_goblet
	material	mat_goblet
	position	0.15 0.36 0.40
}

##################################################
# Lighting -- dusk museum key (warm, from front-
# right-above) + a dim cool fill (the blue hour
# spilling in from a side window) + the votive's
# own small interior light.
##################################################

rect_light
{
	name		light_key
	center		1.60 6.60 5.20
	size		1.60 1.10
	facing		-1.40 -5.65 -5.25
	color		0.98 0.86 0.70
	exitance	380
}

rect_light
{
	name		light_dusk_fill
	center		-2.50 4.50 6.00
	size		2.20 2.60
	facing		2.55 -3.70 -6.05
	color		0.55 0.65 0.90
	exitance	70
}

# Interior votive light: concentric with the shell's inner cavity (local
# offset (0, 0.15, 0) from the shell's own centre), radius 0.09 -- well
# inside the 0.30 cavity radius, so it never pokes through the wall.
shape_light
{
	name		light_votive
	shape		sphere
	center		-0.55 1.01 -0.20
	size		0.09
	color		1.00 0.72 0.32
	exitance	950
}
